2. Modules from RFCs published under BCP 78, before the Trust Legal Provisions
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These RFCs replace the inline grant with a reference: "This document is subject
to the rights, licenses and restrictions contained in BCP 78". The operative
grant is BCP 78 §3.3(a)(E), which names MIB modules explicitly and permits

    to extract, copy, publish, display, distribute, modify and
    incorporate into other works, for any purpose (and not limited to
    use within the IETF Standards Process) any executable code or code
    fragments that are included in any IETF Document (such as MIB and
    PIB modules)

The IETF Trust states in its Frequently Asked Questions
(https://trustee.ietf.org/about/faq/) that this is the licence under which such
modules are used:

    Code Components that are embedded or included in IETF Documents
    published during the period of RFC 5378 effectiveness, March 2005 to
    November 10, 2008, can be used, copied, distributed and modified in
    any manner under the license grant contained in 3.3.a.E.

"In any manner", with no non-commercial restriction and no attribution
condition — so these files are compatible with paid distribution.

The Revised BSD Licence is deliberately *not* cited for this group. It does not
reach a document of this vintage. The Trust Legal Provisions apply to RFCs
published after their effective date, and RFC 5378 §2.1, "No Retroactive Effect",
states that it "does not retroactively obtain additional rights from
Contributions that predate the date that the IETF Trust announces the adoption of
these procedures". The Trust's FAQ draws the same line, putting Revised BSD only
on Code Components published on or after 10 November 2008. The §3.3(a)(E) grant
that does apply here is the broader of the two.

`INET-ADDRESS-MIB` (RFC 4001, February 2005) is dated just before RFC 3978 took
the BCP 78 slot, but the document itself invokes BCP 78, and the RFC occupying
that slot at the time — RFC 3667 — carries §3.3(a)(E) in identical wording, MIB
modules named. The grant covers it either way.

